<h2>How the matter arose</h2>
<p>The magistrate awarded the wife and each of the two minor children Rs 10,000 per month under Section 125 CrPC, payable from the date of the application. Non-payment led to an execution case and, eventually, a warrant of arrest against the husband. He then moved the High Court in revision against both the award and the execution — during court vacation, with the warrant hanging over him.</p>

<h2>The grounds pressed</h2>
<p>Two grounds stood out. First, service: the husband said he had no notice of the maintenance proceeding at all — if true, the award would be effectively ex parte against a party never brought before the court. Second, pleadings versus proof: the wife&rsquo;s application stated his income as Rs 50,000 per month, but her oral evidence escalated it to Rs 80,000; the award, he argued, was built on the larger, unpleaded figure, while his actual income is about Rs 43,675. The court framed the question as whether the magistrate &ldquo;erred in law in relying on evidence beyond the pleadings&rdquo; (para 6), and found a prima facie case for consideration by the regular bench.</p>

<h2>The conditional stay</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>The stay package: all further proceedings in the execution case and the arrest warrant are stayed until 24 December 2026 or further order; the maintenance order is stayed on the same terms; the revisions go to the regular bench in the December 2026 list. The condition: Rs 20,000 per month ad hoc, first instalment by 30 November 2026, all payments without prejudice to both sides.</p>
</div>
<p>The design deserves attention. A bare stay would have left the wife and children with nothing for the months the revision takes; outright refusal would have exposed the husband to arrest on an award he may yet dislodge. The court split the difference at roughly two-thirds of the award — enough to sustain the household, not so much as to pre-judge the revision. This is now the dominant pattern in maintenance revisions across High Courts: interim protection is purchased with interim payment.</p>

<h2>The pleadings point to watch</h2>
<p>The substantive question — can a maintenance court act on an income figure that appears only in oral evidence, not in the pleadings? — has real practical bite. Maintenance proceedings under Section 125 CrPC (now Section 144 BNSS) are summary in nature, and courts routinely say strict pleading rules do not apply with civil-suit rigour. At the same time, a respondent is entitled to know the case he must meet; a figure that grows by sixty per cent between application and deposition invites exactly the challenge raised here. The regular bench&rsquo;s answer will matter to every litigant who faces — or presses — an income claim that evolves mid-proceeding.</p>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>For husbands challenging awards: come with a concrete payment proposal. Courts grant conditional stays, not free ones; an offer near realistic capacity shapes the condition.</li>
  <li>For wives: a revision-stage ad hoc order is money in hand now — insist on a fixed first-payment date and treat default as the trigger to revive execution and the warrant.</li>
  <li>Plead income precisely and amend if better information emerges; the gap between pleaded and deposed figures is the single most exploited seam in maintenance appeals.</li>
  <li>Service objections must be raised at the first opportunity and proved from the record — a bald denial of notice rarely survives scrutiny of the trial court&rsquo;s order sheets.</li>
</ul>
</div>
